!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/resolver-sidecar.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/sidecar-common.tn"
@doc:"""
  Formal shape of a resolver-layer sidecar (tests/class1/resolver/**/*-expected.tn) -- base type
  resolution (§4) over a single bare token. See README.md's "Valid resolver-layer vectors" and
  RUNNER.md.

  This is the one layer with no `error` outcome and so no field group: §4 never rejects a token, it
  always resolves to something (worst case, string). `valid` is therefore a plain REQUIRED field.
  """
{
  number_form_sign  => !enum [plus minus]
  number_form_radix => !enum [hex octal binary]
  special_kind      => !enum [nan infinity]

  exponent => {
    sign: number_form_sign?
    digits: text
  }

  integer_form => {
    sign: number_form_sign?
    digits: text
  }

  based_integer_form => {
    sign: number_form_sign?
    radix: number_form_radix
    digits: text
  }

  float_form => {
    sign: number_form_sign?
    integer-part: text?
    fraction-digits: text?
    exponent: exponent?
  }

  special_value_form => {
    sign: number_form_sign?
    kind: special_kind
  }

  @doc:"""
    One of §7.6's four number-grammar forms. Each shape carries exactly the components it has, so a
    based-integer cannot omit its radix and a float cannot carry one -- the flat, all-optional
    shape this replaces could express both.
    """
  number_form => {
    ( integer: integer_form
    | based-integer: based_integer_form
    | float: float_form
    | special-value: special_value_form )
  }

  @doc:"The decoded text of a token that resolved to the string base type (§4.4)."
  string_value => {
    text: text
  }

  @doc:"""
    The result of base type resolution (§4). `boolean` carries the resolved value; `string` its
    text; `number` a full number_form -- the structure the grammar identified, never a host numeric
    type, which §4.3 leaves to binding.

    There are three members because §4 resolves three classes. Absence is not one of them: `_` is
    lexical, never a token, so no token this layer describes can resolve to it.
    """
  base_value => {
    ( boolean: boolean
    | string: string_value
    | number: number_form )
  }

  resolver_valid => {
    base-value: base_value
  }

  resolver_sidecar => sidecar_common & {
    valid: resolver_valid
  }
}
